<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhatusena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/455">455</a>–473</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhatusena was a king of the Anuradhapura Kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> who reigned from about 455 to 473. He drove out South Indian rulers who had seized the north of the island, restored Buddhist institutions and built great irrigation works, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kala-wewa">Kala Wewa</a> reservoir. He was overthrown and killed by his son Kashyapa I, who later built the rock fortress of Sigiriya.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dhatusena, in some records Dasenkeli, was the first king of the Moriyan dynasty, but his ancestry is uncertain. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Cūḷavaṃsa</a> makes him of royal lineage, descended from a family that had fled the capital some three hundred and seventy-five years before, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a> names his father as Dhata of Ambilayagugama. In 433 Tamil invaders, apparently linked to the Pandyan dynasty and known as the Six Dravidians, killed King Mittasena and took the Anuradhapura Kingdom, while Sinhalese leaders withdrew to Ruhuna in the south. Dhatusena was raised by his uncle, the monk Mahanama, who ordained him to hide him from the invaders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Dhatusena organised a rebellion and claimed the kingship in 455. Three of the six invading rulers had already died fighting among themselves, two more were defeated, and the last, Pithiya, was beaten in a final battle around 459, after which Dhatusena was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>. He confiscated the villages of the invaders' keenest supporters and persuaded those who had fled to Ruhuna to resettle the north.</p><p>He built eighteen large and eighteen small irrigation tanks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kala-wewa">Kala Wewa</a> and Balaluwewa, joined together, cover 6,380 acres, and the Yodha Ela, or Jayaganga, carries water about 54 miles from Kala Wewa to Tissawewa, falling only 95 millimetres in each kilometre. The 13-metre Avukana Buddha statue is attributed to him, as are 18 vihara and lightning rods on the three main dagobas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, and a redaction of the Pali Tipitaka took place in his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His son Kasyapa I was born to a non-royal concubine, while Moggallana I, son of the royal consort, was the rightful heir. When the army commander Migara, Dhatusena's nephew and son-in-law, severely tortured his wife, the king's daughter, after a quarrel, Dhatusena had Migara's mother burned alive. In revenge Migara helped Kasyapa overthrow and imprison the king in 473. Told that his father hid great treasure, Kasyapa had him show it; Dhatusena led him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kala-wewa">Kala Wewa</a>, took up water in his hands and said this was his only treasure. Kasyapa had him walled up alive, or by another account buried in the reservoir's bund, while Moggallana fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhatusena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rice farming depended on great reservoirs like Kala Wewa, built by <b>Dhatusena</b>, and ports traded with India, Rome and China.</blockquote><a href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
